Let $C$ be the cost to inflate one tire, which is 25 cents or $0.25$.

On the first day, 5 people on bicycles came by to get both tires inflated. This means $5 \times 2 = 10$ tires were inflated.
The cost for inflating these tires is $10 \times 0.25 = 2.50$ dollars.

3 people came by to get all their tricycle tires inflated. A tricycle has 3 tires, so $3 \times 3 = 9$ tires were inflated.
The cost for inflating these tires is $9 \times 0.25 = 2.25$ dollars.

One person came by on a unicycle. A unicycle has 1 tire, so $1 \times 1 = 1$ tire was inflated.
The cost for inflating this tire is $1 \times 0.25 = 0.25$ dollars.

The total number of tires inflated is $10 + 9 + 1 = 20$.
The total cost is $2.50 + 2.25 + 0.25 = 5.00$ dollars.

Alternatively,
Let $B$ be the number of bicycles, $T$ be the number of tricycles, and $U$ be the number of unicycles.
We have $B=5$, $T=3$, and $U=1$.
Each bicycle has 2 tires, so $5 \times 2 = 10$ bicycle tires are inflated.
Each tricycle has 3 tires, so $3 \times 3 = 9$ tricycle tires are inflated.
The unicycle has 1 tire, so $1 \times 1 = 1$ unicycle tire is inflated.
Total number of tires inflated = $10 + 9 + 1 = 20$ tires.
Each tire costs 25 cents to inflate, so the total cost is $20 \times 0.25 = 5$ dollars.

The total money Shawnda made that day is $5.00$ dollars.

